Title: alfe's AutoCAD automation mode never quits the acad.exe it starts
Status: open
Found: 2026-09-15 (pjb: "CI jobs on the windows runner leave autocad open,
       with a Dessin1.dwg, and no activity")
Related: issues/open/cad-runner-wedged-by-modal-dialog.issue ("the engine
         outliving its job")

SYMPTOM

After CI jobs on the Windows laptop runner (PF5S26B), AutoCAD stays open
on a default new drawing -- "Dessin1.dwg" -- doing nothing.

IT IS NOT A SCRIPT THAT FORGETS _QUIT, AND IT IS NOT CANCELLED JOBS.
Every alfe run in AutoCAD AUTOMATION mode (GUI acad.exe driven through
COM) leaves AutoCAD running, successful runs included. Two independent
lines of evidence agree.

CAUSE, from the code (master 2e5657b5)

 1. scripts/run-vendor-probes.ps1 defaults -Dwg to "", so for AutoCAD it
    calls `alfe --no-init --autocad -l PROBE` with no --mode; --mode
    batch is only added for BricsCAD or when a drawing is given.
    run-path-sysvars-probe.ps1:72, run-getcname-probe.ps1 and
    run-pgp-probe.ps1:42 pass `--mode automation` explicitly.

 2. With no mode, CHOOSE-EFFECTIVE-MODE (backend-autocad.lisp:451-457)
    picks :AUTOMATION whenever acad.exe is found; :batch is only the
    fallback.

 3. Automation does not run AutoCAD: it runs
    `cscript //nologo bridge-autocad.vbs` (backend-autocad.lisp:468-470).
    The VBScript tries GetObject(, "AutoCAD.Application"), else
    CreateObject; sets app.Visible = True; and calls
    app.Documents.Add("") when nothing is open -- which is Dessin1.dwg,
    since a drawing is only resolved for :batch (:463-466).

 4. It then exits with:
       ' Don't call app.Quit here - the runtime publishes its own status
       ' and the alfe-side poller decides on the lifecycle.
       WScript.Quit 0

 5. The alfe side never decides it. On SHUTDOWN the in-AutoCAD runtime
    only sets STOPPING and a stop flag (runtime/autolisp-remote-io.lsp:
    136-139); the server loop reports STOPPED and returns. SHUTDOWN in
    backend-autocad.lisp:846-863 then calls KILL-ENGINE-PROCESS on the
    process it launched -- which is CSCRIPT, not AutoCAD. The
    start-abort UNWIND-PROTECT (:742-755) kills the same cscript.

 6. The quit machinery exists and is dead: AUTOLISP-FINISH
    (runtime/autolisp-bootstrap.lsp:207) would call AUTOLISP-HOST-QUIT
    -> (command "_QUIT" "_Y") when *AUTOLISP_QUIT_ON_FINISH*, and
    NOTHING CALLS AUTOLISP-FINISH.

 7. The specification asked for app.Quit gated on `created And
    statusReadyFlag`, plus a taskkill helper
    (documentation/alfe--specifications.org:1303-1340,
    issues/closed/alfe-backend-autocad.issue:59,67-69). Neither was
    implemented.

A COM-activated acad.exe is started by Windows' COM service, not as a
child of cscript, alfe or the job shell. So no job ending -- success,
failure, cancel, timeout -- takes it down, and a whole-process-tree kill
from the job would not reach it either.

EVIDENCE, from 10 days of CI (2026-09-05 to 09-15, 1,758 jobs on PF5S26B)

WARM STARTS ARE THE PROOF. In 100 of 108 vendor:probes:autocad runs the
FIRST alfe run reached READY in ~0.4-0.8 s -- AutoCAD was ALREADY
running when the job began. Only 5 were cold 11-15 s starts. Within one
job, job 16507445872:

    11:12:16  backend AUTOCAD: READY after 11.50 s   (cold: CreateObject)
    11:12:19  [CAD] server-loop returned normally
    11:12:20  backend AUTOCAD: READY after 0.38 s    (warm: GetObject)
    11:12:24  backend AUTOCAD: READY after 0.37 s
    11:12:28  backend AUTOCAD: READY after 0.38 s

No shutdown, kill or quit line for AutoCAD appears in any trace.

THE KILL KILLS CSCRIPT. Job 16333910198, 09-06:

    spawned, process-info-pid = 23116
    READY timeout after 60.06 s; last status = "BOOTING"
    start aborted; terminating spawned engine (pid 23116)
    ... later:
    READY after 53.71 s
    READY after 0.78 s

pid 23116 was cscript; the AutoCAD that COM started carried on booting
and was attached to later.

CANCELS AND TIMEOUTS ARE NOT THE MECHANISM. Only ONE GUI-mode AutoCAD
job was cancelled mid-script in ten days (16463865287); no job ended in
job_execution_timeout; the one 1800 s AutoCAD job hung in `git fetch`
before any CAD ran. (This was worth checking: pushes had cancelled many
Windows jobs during the probe work, and it would have been an easy,
wrong explanation.)

AFFECTED JOBS: the four automation-mode ones -- vendor:probes:autocad,
path-sysvars:probe:autocad:windows, getcname:probe:autocad:windows,
pgp:probe:autocad:windows. The batch AutoCAD jobs (encoding, pathname,
probe:suite, every *:accoreconsole) use accoreconsole.exe with a drawing
and `._QUIT _Y`, and cannot show a Dessin1 window.

SIDE EFFECTS ALREADY SEEN, likely consequences of a persistent instance:
  - jobs 16389774660 and 16442007951 failed with "L'appel a été rejeté
    par l'appelé" -- COM call rejected by callee -- i.e. the leftover
    AutoCAD was busy or showing a dialog when a new job attached to it;
  - GetObject attaches to ANY running AutoCAD, including one a human
    opened on the laptop, and the probes would then run inside it.

FIX -- two parts, and two decisions that are pjb's

 A. QUIT WHAT WE CREATED, AND ONLY THAT. The bridge already records
    `created` versus `attached` (EmitFlags). Quitting an ATTACHED
    instance could close a session pjb has open on that laptop. For a
    CREATED one: close each document WITHOUT saving, then app.Quit,
    through COM. Not (command "_QUIT" "_Y"): Dessin1 is unnamed, and a
    save prompt there is itself a modal-dialog hang.

    Decision: should an instance the bridge ATTACHED to be left alone
    (safe for a human user) or also quit (tidier for an unattended
    runner)?

 B. A RUNNER-SIDE SAFETY NET for the endings alfe cannot see -- a killed
    alfe, a cancelled job -- since a COM-activated acad.exe is outside
    the job's process tree. The laptop is also pjb's own machine, so a
    blanket `taskkill /IM acad.exe` could kill his AutoCAD. The safe
    scope is acad.exe whose command line contains /Automation, which a
    hand-started AutoCAD does not have:
      Get-CimInstance Win32_Process -Filter "Name='acad.exe'" |
        Where-Object CommandLine -match '/Automation'

    Decision: is that scoping acceptable on the laptop, and should it
    run before each job, after each job, or both?

 Also worth deciding: vendor:probes:autocad reaches automation only by
 DEFAULT (no -Dwg, no --mode). If those probes do not need the GUI,
 `--mode batch` removes this job from the affected set outright.

WHY NOT FIXED IN THE SAME CHANGE: part A is a COM change that can only
be verified on a Windows host with AutoCAD, and both decisions above
change what happens on a machine a human uses.
